# .env.cloud.example
#
# The free-cloud deployment topology (see DEPLOYMENT-CLOUD.md): Postgres on
# Neon, Redis on Upstash, Kafka on Aiven, RabbitMQ on CloudAMQP, the app on
# Render. Everything NOT in this file (SMTP, PII keys, JWT secrets, role API
# keys, provider keys, CORS_ORIGINS, CONSENT_ENFORCEMENT, ...) is unchanged
# from .env.example — paste those in as normal. This file only covers what's
# DIFFERENT when the infra is managed/cloud instead of the bundled containers.
#
# render.yaml at the repo root is the authoritative list of every variable
# Render's Blueprint deploy needs and how each is supplied (fixed, prompted,
# or auto-generated) — this file is the reference for the VALUES and where to
# find them, not a substitute for it. Paste the resulting values into
# Render's Blueprint prompt (or dashboard → your service → Environment for a
# manual deploy). Never commit a filled-in copy of this file.

# App — Render sets PORT itself; NODE_ENV should be production.
NODE_ENV=production

# Postgres — Neon dashboard → your project → Connection Details → "Pooled
# connection", which already includes ?sslmode=require. Paste it whole.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://<user>:<password>@<host>.neon.tech/<db>?sslmode=require

# Redis — Upstash console → your database → Details. Use the TCP endpoint
# (host/port), NOT the REST URL — this app talks the Redis protocol via
# ioredis, not Upstash's HTTP API.
REDIS_HOST=<endpoint>.upstash.io
REDIS_PORT=6379
REDIS_PASSWORD=<password from the same page>
REDIS_TLS=true

# Kafka — Upstash discontinued Upstash Kafka in March 2025 (no longer in
# their console). Use Aiven's free Kafka plan instead: Service → Overview
# for Host/Port and all three certs below. Real Kafka wire protocol.
#
# Aiven's free plan uses mutual TLS, not SASL — confirmed empirically: a
# plain-SSL listener never expects a SASL handshake, and sending one anyway
# fails with "Request is not valid given the current SASL state" even with
# correct credentials. So KAFKA_SASL_USERNAME/PASSWORD stay unset here; the
# app skips SASL entirely once a client cert is configured (see
# kafka.service.ts). They still exist in this app's config for a provider
# whose service genuinely is SASL_SSL (a paid Aiven plan, Confluent, etc.).
KAFKA_BROKERS=<host>:<port>
KAFKA_SSL=true
# All three from the same Overview page. Base64 each — genuinely one line,
# so there's nothing for a web form to mangle on paste (raw multi-line PEM
# also works, the app auto-detects either form, but base64 can't go wrong):
#   base64 -i ca.pem | tr -d '\n'                  (macOS; base64 -w0 on Linux)
#
# Verify before touching Render at all — the real connection, independent
# of the app entirely:
#   openssl s_client -connect <host>:<port> -CAfile ca.pem \
#     -cert service.cert -key service.key -brief
# "Verification: OK" with the connection staying open (Ctrl+C to exit — that
# IS success, s_client just waits for interactive input) means it works.
KAFKA_SSL_CA=<base64 of ca.pem>
KAFKA_SSL_CLIENT_CERT=<base64 of the Access Certificate>
KAFKA_SSL_CLIENT_KEY=<base64 of the Access Key>
# Topics must exist before use (allowAutoTopicCreation is already false in
# kafka.config.ts) — only 3 of the 7 names defined there are actually used
# (routing/delivery/status/analytics are reserved, unused today); create just
# these three in the console — comfortably inside Aiven's free-plan 5-topic cap:
# notification-events, notification-critical, notification-dlq

# RabbitMQ — CloudAMQP console → your instance → AMQP URL (starts amqps://,
# already includes credentials and the vhost). Paste it whole.
RABBITMQ_URL=amqps://<user>:<password>@<host>.cloudamqp.com/<vhost>

# Runs `prisma migrate deploy` once before the app starts (see
# docker/entrypoint.sh) — Render has no equivalent to Compose's one-shot
# `migrate` service. Set this on Render only; never set it in local .env.
RUN_MIGRATIONS_ON_BOOT=true

# CORS — your Vercel frontend's actual URL(s), comma-separated, no trailing slash.
CORS_ORIGINS=https://your-frontend.vercel.app
